# StaySync — .cursorrules (Java Swing Edition)
# Rules for AI coding agents (Cursor / Antigravity). If your tool doesn't read `.cursorrules`,
# paste this file into its rules/instructions area.

## 0. Project context
StaySync (formal title: "Hostel Mess Complaint Tracker") is a DBSC database microproject.
Students report hostel issues; admins triage and resolve them.

**Stack is FIXED:** Java 21 · **Java Swing** GUI (FlatLaf) · Java application logic · **JDBC** (MySQL Connector/J + HikariCP) · **MySQL 8** (`staysync_db`, 7 tables) · MySQL Workbench for DB work.
**FORBIDDEN:** React, JavaScript, TypeScript, HTML/CSS, Node/npm, web frameworks, Spring/Spring Boot, REST APIs, JPA/Hibernate, JavaFX, WebView/JCEF/embedded browsers. If you think one is needed, STOP and ask.

Source of truth, in order: `docs/PRD.md` → `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` → `docs/DESIGN.md`. Read the relevant doc BEFORE building a feature. If code and docs disagree, stop and ask — don't silently diverge.
The UI is inspired by wisprflow.ai's craft (clean type, whitespace, refined color, polished cards, subtle motion). NEVER copy its text, logo, images, or exact layout.

## 1. Working agreements
- Work in small vertical slices (screen + controller + service + DAO + SQL + tests). Follow milestones M0→M7 in the PRD.
- Before coding a feature, list the files you will touch. After, list what changed and how to verify it.
- Don't add dependencies without saying why; prefer those in `ARCHITECTURE.md` §2. Verify third-party class/method names against the pinned version — don't guess APIs.
- Don't change table/column names or the schema without updating `database/01_schema.sql` AND `ARCHITECTURE.md` Appendix A.
- Never invent requirements, statistics, testimonials, certifications, or customer logos.
- If a request is ambiguous or risky (security, schema, confidentiality, threading), ask one concise question first.
- Keep changes minimal and focused; don't refactor unrelated code in the same change.

## 2. Code style
- Java 21: records for models/DTOs, `enum`s for Role/Status/Priority, `var` only when the type is obvious, `Optional` for "maybe absent" returns (never return null collections).
- Constructor injection by hand (no DI framework). Services and DAOs are plain classes wired in one place (`App`/`ServiceRegistry`).
- Packages: `config, db, model, dao, service, security, error, ui.{theme,motion,components,charts,shell,screens}, util`. Classes `PascalCase`, methods `camelCase`, constants `UPPER_SNAKE`.
- SQL: UPPERCASE keywords, snake_case identifiers, tables plural. Keep SQL in DAOs (text blocks `"""`), never in UI or services.
- Comments explain *why*. No commented-out code. No `System.out.println` — use SLF4J.
- Run the formatter and tests before finishing.

## 3. THREADING (highest-frequency bug — obey strictly)
- **EDT only** for creating/mutating Swing components, painting, and listeners. Start the UI with `SwingUtilities.invokeLater`.
- **Never** run on the EDT: JDBC, file I/O, BCrypt, image decoding/scaling, CSV writing, `Thread.sleep`, network. Use `SwingWorker` or the shared `Async.run(work, onOk, onErr)` helper; update the UI in `onOk/onErr` (EDT).
- Every screen shows a `SkeletonPanel` while loading and an `ErrorBanner` (with retry) on failure.
- Drop stale responses (per-controller request counter) so older filter results never overwrite newer ones.
- Timers/animators: one per concern, stopped when the component is hidden or removed (`removeNotify`/`HierarchyListener`). No leaks.
- Background poller for notifications: single daemon `ScheduledExecutorService`; results posted via `invokeLater`.

## 4. Swing & UI rules
- Look and feel: **FlatLaf only** (Light/Dark). Never mix Metal/Nimbus. Theme switch uses the animated change helper; no restart.
- **Tokens only:** read colors/fonts/radii/spacing through `Tokens`/`Fonts` (backed by `FlatLightLaf.properties`/`FlatDarkLaf.properties`). **No hard-coded `new Color(...)`, hex strings, `new Font(...)`, or magic pixel sizes in components.** Custom painting must multiply by `UIScale.scale(...)`.
- Layout: **MigLayout/BorderLayout/GridBagLayout. No `setLayout(null)`, no absolute `setBounds` for managed content.** Must resize cleanly from 1000×680 up to 4K and at 100–200% scaling.
- Custom components extend `JComponent`/`JPanel`, paint with `Graphics2D` (antialiasing on), call `super.paintComponent`, set `setOpaque` correctly, and expose accessible names/roles.
- Cache expensive rendering (aurora gradients, shadows, scaled thumbnails) as `BufferedImage`; invalidate on resize/theme change. Repaint only the affected rectangle.
- Animation: only through `Motion` helpers (FlatLaf `Animator` or a 16 ms `javax.swing.Timer`); animate paint-level properties (alpha, offset, progress); **do not animate layout** (except the sidebar collapse helper). Durations from motion tokens, ≤ 480 ms inside the app. Everything non-essential must honor `Motion.enabled()` (Reduce motion).
- Modals are in-window (`ModalLayer` on the glass pane) with focus containment and `Esc` to close; use `JOptionPane` only for fatal errors.
- Status/priority/confidential are never color-only: chip = dot/bars/shield + text.
- Every interactive control: keyboard reachable, visible focus, accessible name/description, sensible mnemonic or shortcut; `Enter` = default action, `Esc` = cancel.
- Tables: `JTable` with models holding only the current page; server-side sort/filter/pagination; custom renderers; tooltips for truncated text (never confidential text in tooltips for unauthorized users).
- Mixed-font headings use `EmphasisHeading` (TextLayout-based), not side-by-side labels.
- Never put sensitive complaint text in window titles, toasts, tooltips, notifications (confidential), or logs.

## 5. Database & JDBC rules
- **`PreparedStatement` only. NEVER build SQL by concatenating user data.** Not for `LIKE`, not for `ORDER BY`, not for `LIMIT`. Sort columns come from a whitelist map; escape `%`/`_` in `LIKE` terms; fixed SQL fragments only when building dynamic filters, with values in a bind list.
- Use try-with-resources for `Connection`, `PreparedStatement`, `ResultSet`. Get connections from the HikariCP `DataSource`; never keep a connection in a field.
- Multi-table writes happen in ONE service-level transaction via `TransactionRunner` (`setAutoCommit(false)` → commit/rollback). DAO methods used in a transaction take a `Connection` parameter.
- Every status or priority change MUST write a `complaint_history` row and a `notifications` row **in the same transaction**.
- Enforce transitions in the service: Pending→In Progress, In Progress→Resolved, Resolved→In Progress (reopen, note required). The DB trigger is a backstop, not the primary check.
- Resolving requires non-blank `resolution_notes` and sets `resolved_at`; reopening clears it.
- Concurrency: `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, then `UPDATE … WHERE complaint_id=? AND updated_at=?`; 0 rows ⇒ `ConflictException`.
- Never hard-delete complaints. Deactivate categories instead of deleting.
- Map DB errors to typed exceptions; never show SQL text or stack traces to users.
- The app connects as `staysync_app` (SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE only) — **never `root`.** Schema changes are made in MySQL Workbench, not by app code.
- Seed/demo data is synthetic. Keep SQL scripts in `database/` runnable top-to-bottom in MySQL Workbench; add report-worthy queries to `04_queries.sql`.

## 6. CONFIDENTIALITY (highest-priority rule)
Safety/ragging/harassment complaints (any complaint with `is_confidential = TRUE`) must never reach unauthorized users through ANY path: tables, search, counts, charts, CSV, notifications, attachments, tooltips, logs.
- Admin-side reads/writes MUST use the shared confidentiality predicate helper (`AND (c.is_confidential = FALSE OR ? = TRUE)` bound to `canViewConfidential`). Do not hand-write variants.
- Analytics read from `v_general_complaints`, except the explicit Authorized-Admin confidential summary (counts only).
- Hidden or unauthorized records raise `NotFoundException` — same user-facing text as a missing record.
- Notification text for confidential complaints is generic ("There's an update on your private request."): no title, description, or category name.
- Attachments for confidential complaints are encrypted at rest (AES-GCM) and opened only through `AttachmentService` after an authorization check.
- Any new method touching complaint data requires a confidentiality test in the same change.
- The Safety category UI always shows the immediate-danger notice.

## 7. Security checklist (apply to every service method)
1. First line: `Permissions.require…(Session.current())`.
2. Does the SQL `WHERE` verify ownership (students) or scope/predicate (admins)? (No IDOR.)
3. Are inputs validated (length, format, enums) in the service, even if the UI validated them?
4. Could an error or "not found" message reveal that a hidden record exists?
5. Passwords: BCrypt; never log, display, or store plaintext; never return `password_hash`.
6. Registration always creates `Role.STUDENT`; ignore any role input. Admins only via seed or `UserAdminService` (Authorized Admin).
7. Uploads: ≤ 5 MB, ≤ 3 files, decode with `ImageIO` (null ⇒ reject), re-encode to strip EXIF, UUID filenames, delete files on rollback.
8. CSV exports: neutralize cells starting `=`, `+`, `-`, `@`, tab, CR; RFC 4180 quoting; UTF-8 BOM; row cap.
9. Secrets: `config/app.properties` and `uploads/` are git-ignored. **No credentials or keys in source, tests, or docs.**
10. Idle auto-lock and logout must fully clear `Session`; no sensitive data persisted to disk or `Preferences`.

## 8. Design rules (from DESIGN.md)
- Minimal, premium, calm. Generous whitespace, one accent color, one primary action per screen.
- Fonts: Inter (UI), JetBrains Mono (IDs/numbers), Instrument Serif *italic* only for emphasis words (Welcome headline, success/empty titles).
- Welcome screen = split layout: aurora stage with scripted hero demo (messy text → ticket card cycling Pending → In Progress → Resolved) + sign-in card with **Students | Admins** segmented control.
- Hero demo is a **scripted illustration, not AI**; never claim automatic categorization in the product.
- Dashboards use StatCards (count-up), Card grids, DataTable + PaginationBar, Timeline, ConversationView, custom Java2D charts (draw-in once).
- Both themes must look intentional; check contrast ≥ 4.5:1 for text.
- Empty, loading (skeleton), error (banner + retry), and success states are required on every screen.

## 9. Testing and "definition of done"
A task is done only when:
- [ ] It satisfies the cited PRD requirement ID(s) (put them in the commit message).
- [ ] Service/DAO tests cover rules, transactions/rollback, and authorization; confidentiality test added if complaint data is touched.
- [ ] SQL verified in MySQL Workbench; report-relevant SQL saved in `database/`.
- [ ] UI: both themes; 1000×680, 1280×800, 1920×1080; 100%/150% scaling; keyboard-only path works; Reduce motion verified.
- [ ] No DB or file I/O on the EDT (verify with an artificial 3 s DB delay: window must still repaint).
- [ ] No hard-coded colors/fonts/sizes; no lint/format warnings; app builds and starts.
- [ ] Docs updated if behavior, schema, or service API changed.
Never claim something works without running it. If you couldn't run it, say so plainly.

## 10. Git
- Branch per slice: `feat/sub-submit-screen`, `fix/conf-export-leak`.
- Conventional commits referencing PRD IDs: `feat(complaint): create complaint with attachments [SUB-1..4]`.
- Never commit `config/app.properties`, `uploads/`, `logs/`, `target/`, IDE files, or real credentials.

## 11. Things you must NOT do
- Don't introduce any web technology, Spring, JPA/Hibernate, or JavaFX (see §0).
- Don't touch the DB or files on the EDT.
- Don't use `Statement` with concatenated SQL or `root` credentials.
- Don't expose confidential data "temporarily for debugging".
- Don't add admin self-registration.
- Don't implement Non-Goals (email/SMS, voice, AI classification, multi-tenant, web/mobile).
- Don't fabricate testimonials, metrics, logos, or compliance badges.
- Don't copy wisprflow.ai assets or copy.
- Don't use `setLayout(null)` or pixel-fixed layouts.

## 12. When unsure
Stop and ask. Offer 2–3 concrete options with a recommendation. A short question beats a large wrong change.
